A||N||4||ElricSI||Saw and Indy on the road today||||10||
Z||000000||ElricSI||04-02-2001||08:22 PM||||I could see it was a Fiero from way back, but I practically crapped myself when I pulled up next to it (in my Volvo, so I couldn't beep or anything.)  I don't think I have EVER seen an indy on the road before.<P>What is the rarest type of Fiero that is still on the road?  Indy, Formula, 85 2M6?  Anyone have any idea?<P>------------------<BR>Proud Owner of 85 GT||||reg||10
Z||000001||Kelvin Vivian||04-03-2001||12:00 AM||||Depends on how far you want to break down the categories.... (*whips out his handy Fiero Production stats*)<P>84 Indy Pace Car: 2,000<BR>84 Base Coupe: 7,099<BR>84 Sport Coupe: 62,070<BR>84 SE: 65,671<BR>Total 84 Production: 136,843<P>85 Base Coupe: 5,280<BR>85 Sport Coupe: 23,823<BR>85 SE 4-cyl: 12,901<BR>85 SE V6: 11,833<BR>85 GT: 22,534<BR>Total 85 Production: 76,371<P>86 Base Coupe: 9,143<BR>86 Sport Coupe: 24,866<BR>86 SE 4-cyl: 4,031<BR>86 SE V6: 28,274<BR>86 GT: 17,655<BR>Total 86 Production: 83,969<P>87 Base Coupe: 23,603<BR>87 Sport Coupe: 3,135<BR>87 SE 4-cyl: 1,401<BR>87 SE V6: 2,474<BR>87 GT: 15,966<BR>Total 87 Production: 46,579<P>88 Coupe: 13,910<BR>88 Formula: 5,684<BR>88 GT: 6,849<BR>Total 88 Production: 24,601<P>Total Fiero Production ~368,363<P>Judging by the number of Fieros in Pick-N-Pull lots across America, I'd say 10% of Fieros are no longer running.<P>My guess would be that 84 Indy's are the rarest on the street... 2000 made - 10%(2000) = 1800 Indys still running.<P>Next would be an 87 Sport Coupe, 3135 made - 10%(3135) = 2821 87 SCs still running...<P>However, if you want to break it down further, as by your questioning an 85 2M6, then I would say that an 87 SE 4-cyl would be the rarest still running at, 1400 made - 10%(1400) = 1260 87 SE 4-cyls<P>[This message has been edited by Kelvin Vivian (edited 04-03-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by Kelvin Vivian (edited 04-03-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Kelvin Vivian (edited 04-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||wkayl||04-03-2001||12:38 AM||||Here is one on E-bay<BR> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=574352622&r=0&t=0" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=574352622&r=0&t=0</A> <P>If anyone wants me to check it out it is only abour 10 miles from me.  I am sure it is the one I saw at BK here in Loveland last fall.  As the description says, it looks good but not a show winner.  It had a very dirty engine compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ElricSI||04-05-2001||12:39 AM||||I'd say a whole lot more than 10% are not running anymore.  I have the spotter's guide too, but I wondered if anyone had best guesses based on their general Fiero knowledge.  I'd bet 84's are MUCH more likely not to be running anymore.  Plus people seem to give up on the 4's but they are always trying to keep 6's running.  An Indy Fiero is an appealing idea, but I would probably be bored with it since I have the same body style with a better engine.<P>------------------<BR>Proud Owner of 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||04-05-2001||12:50 AM||||I'd say a lot of Fieros are still running.. just don't look like Fieros anymore...<P>And as far as being boring because you can have the same car with a better engine.... sure thing! but it still ain't a Pace Car! There is something to be said for that...<P>Kinda like my Monte Carlo SS Aerocoupe.. Same car and same engine as the regular Monte Carlo SS, but with that big (what most people consider ugly) rear window! That rear window is the only reason I have it! Why? 1)It makes it a rare car. 2) It means NASCAR!<P><BR>To quote ElricSI.. people don't "almost crap themselves" every time they see a Fiero or a Monte Carlo SS... But make it an Indy Pace Car Fiero or a Monte Carlo SS Aerocoupe and people really get excited... Rare is good!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
